                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Sound design is mediocre, and I'm not saying this just to insult. Here is my feedback.\n\nFirstly, when looking at proper sound design you need six things to be in shape.\n-Panning: sound direction between left and right output.\n-Balance: Prioritiztion of sound, what needs to be heard. Usually dialogue in top priority\n-Equalization: Managing your frequencies. Bass-treble Low-High. \n-Dynamics: When things are loud or soft and when they need to be loud or soft\n-Levels: Technical standpoint of the waves output level. In this games case it is too much, the sound clips often\n-Dimensions: Reverb, Delay. This directly correlates to atmosphere in parrallel with everything above\n\nFootsteps: They are very dry, and their volume should be lowered slightly. They need a touch of reverb in order to give them that metalic room vibe and overall this should help in atmosphere.\n\nAtmosphere: From what I've heard, there is none. A consistant deep bass drone tone is not going to give you any form of atmosphere especially with how random and inappropriate its placement is. I never feel like I am in the rooms that I am actually in. This is as simple as going into a room with the desired sound, recording the silence of the room, then placing that sound file to play in the corresponding rooms of the game\n\nDoor Sounds: Clipping... Lower the output level of the sound or use a better soundwave. P.s. Doors make sounds when they finish closing or opening, one electronic sound wont make the cut.\n\nPistol: Sounds cartoonish, like a stock sound even. I suggest making its pitch higher and shortening the decay so the sound doesnt play so long. Or jsu tuse a different sound altogether.\n\nFlamethrower: sounds good.\n\nNow for the Game Design aspect.\n\nQuestiosn to ask yourself when making a game. \n\n\"Why is this fun?\" If you cannot answer this question quickly, then odds are you aren't making a fun game. This game gave me the same vibe as a previous game I had worked with \"Space Beast Terror Fright\" and that game was adrenaline filled and action packed and kept you on your toes. This felt like a slower, less interesting version.\n\nMovement: I actually really like the movement in this game, the character feels like they have weight, walking feels good, aiming feels good. Character leans in a minimal fashion, and in general I feel like a person, not a video game character.\n\nEnemies: enemies walking in a straight line is not interesting unless the gameplay is built around it. These little bugs should be on walls, jumping, scurrying, quick abript movements. not slowly walking towards you.\n\nWHAT I WANT TO SEE\n\nI see this game uses the same assets and such from a previosu title you made. That title has mixed results, and makign a game that looks the same will also leave you with mixed results. The visuals dont look improved, the sounds are reused, and It's not somethign people will look at and say \"I want more of that\"\n\nI would rather see what more you guys can do, as I see potential in how you make your games. there is definitely some knowhow going on but it needs more work and application. \n\nMy advice, don't stop making games.",
